# ── Weirvern configuration ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env and edit. NEVER commit .env.

# ClickHouse
CLICKHOUSE_HOST=clickhouse
CLICKHOUSE_PORT=8123
CLICKHOUSE_DB=weirvern
# The cluster serves user `weirvern` (k8s/20-configmap-env.yaml); `default` is locked
# to localhost by the official ClickHouse entrypoint, so a local run must not use it.
CLICKHOUSE_USER=weirvern
CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=
# Timezone used for window calculations (also sets ClickHouse TZ)
TZ=America/Chicago

# Collector
# (No port knobs on purpose -- see the note in k8s/20-configmap-env.yaml: the listen ports
#  are literals in k8s/31-goflow2.yaml and install.sh, and knobs nothing reads were removed.
#  Edit those two files together to move a port.)

# Zeek packet sensor (second view of the network: DNS/TLS/conn behaviour).
#
# TWO INTERFACES, TWO JOBS. Use a second NIC for capture; do not sniff the
# management port.
#
#   ZEEK_IFACE  the CAPTURE port -- a dedicated NIC fed by a switch SPAN/mirror
#               port or a passive TAP. It must not carry the default route and
#               must not be a member of a bridge.
#   MGMT_IFACE  the MANAGEMENT port -- default route, SSH, cluster API. Not sniffed.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS: a mirror port receives copies of other hosts' traffic. The
# management port sees only traffic to and from this host plus broadcast noise, so
# sniffing it yields a capture that looks busy and shows you no network at all.
# Measured on a real deployment: 73% broadcast/multicast, no unicast from other
# hosts.
#
# List the interfaces a node actually has, with a verdict on each:
#     weirvern interfaces --node <sensor-node>
ZEEK_IFACE=eth1
# The management port: default route, SSH, cluster API. Not sniffed. Set this so
# Weirvern can tell you when ZEEK_IFACE points at the wrong NIC.
MGMT_IFACE=eth0
# Where the worker reads Zeek logs (path inside the worker container).
ZEEK_LOG_DIR=/zeek/logs

# ── In-path intercept (siphon mode only) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Everything below is used ONLY by `weirvern intercept`, the mode that makes
# Weirvern a ROUTER on your network. Leave them unset unless you run siphon mode;
# nothing else reads them.
#
#   INTERCEPT_SEGMENT  the CIDR whose traffic to relay, e.g. 198.51.100.0/24
#   INTERCEPT_ADDR     the address to take ON THAT SEGMENT so the kernel can route
#                      and forward there. It is added when the intercept starts and
#                      withdrawn on every exit path, including failure and Ctrl-C.
#                      Verify it is free first: `weirvern intercept --dry-run`
#                      reports the blast radius and offers addresses that answered
#                      no ARP.
#   INTERCEPT_GATEWAY  the gateway whose address gets forged. Must be the REAL
#                      gateway for that segment: interception needs its true MAC to
#                      restore the mapping afterwards.
#
# WHY NOT DHCP: a lease can change or be withdrawn mid-intercept, and that is the
# one failure the watchdog cannot tell from idleness -- the forwarding counter
# simply stops advancing, so poisoned hosts keep believing Weirvern is the gateway
# while it can no longer forward for them. A static address verified free is a
# change the operator chose and that teardown can undo deterministically.
#
# These live here rather than in the cluster ConfigMap because siphon mode runs from
# the host or a NET_ADMIN-equipped pod, not from the passive sensor's config.
# INTERCEPT_SEGMENT=198.51.100.0/24
# INTERCEPT_ADDR=198.51.100.250
# INTERCEPT_GATEWAY=198.51.100.1

# ── Sensor mode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOW Weirvern gets its packets. Named for the creature: a wyvern keeps its water
# in a LOCH and draws it through a SIPHON.
#
#   loch  PASSIVE (default). Still water: Zeek listens on a promiscuous NIC fed by
#         a switch SPAN/mirror port or a passive TAP, and observes. Weirvern cannot
#         affect traffic because it is not in the path. If the sensor dies you lose
#         visibility, nothing else.
#
#   siphon  IN-PATH INTERCEPT. A conduit the water is drawn THROUGH: Weirvern
#         ARP-spoofs selected hosts so their traffic is relayed through it,
#         capturing as it passes. Needs no switch, router or DHCP change — but
#         Weirvern becomes a ROUTER, and every packet waits on it. The whole flow
#         is drawn through the siphon; when it stops, the flow stops with it. It
#         requires CAP_NET_ADMIN, three sysctls on the node, and the fail-open
#         watchdog.
#
# Check what a mode would require before enabling it:  weirvern mode siphon
SENSOR_MODE=loch

# siphon mode only: WHO to intercept. Comma-separated addresses or CIDRs, or * for
# every host on the capture segment. EMPTY MEANS NOTHING IS INTERCEPTED, even in
# siphon mode — turning on the mode does not by itself divert any traffic. The
# targets are a separate, deliberate decision.
#
# Never list: the management gateway, this sensor's own address, the Kubernetes
# API server, or the sensor's DNS resolver. Intercepting those breaks the tool
# with the tool.
INTERCEPT_TARGETS=

# Detection loop
DETECT_INTERVAL_S=300          # sweep cadence (seconds)
RETRAIN_EVERY_H=6              # full retrain cadence
RETRAIN_WINDOW_H=72            # training window (3 days)

# Retention: ONE window for every table the detection and ripple layers read -- raw and
# windowed flows, the Zeek conn/dns/http/ssl/ssh/notice/weird logs, the identity channels the
# ripple layer reads, and its evidence store.
#
# "auto" DERIVES the window from the free space on ClickHouse's disk and the ingest rate those
# tables have actually measured, at startup and on every apply. The row in Settings shows the
# arithmetic, and it re-derives as the disk fills -- nobody edits a day count. An explicit
# 7-365 overrides it and is refused above what the disk can hold.
#
# DATA_RETENTION_RATIO is the share of FREE space the tables may occupy (default 40%, 10-70).
# The rest is merge headroom: ClickHouse needs room to compact, and a table budgeted to the
# last byte stops accepting inserts exactly when it is biggest.
# LOWERING EITHER ONE EXPIRES OLDER ROWS on ClickHouse's next merge, permanently.
DATA_RETENTION_DAYS=auto      # "auto" or 7-365 days
DATA_RETENTION_RATIO=40       # % of free disk; 10-70, the rest is merge headroom
BASE_THRESHOLD=0.35            # ensemble score floor
MIN_AGREEMENT=0.33             # fraction of models that must fire
STRONG_SIGNAL=0.85             # detector score that counts as a strong signal
TAIL_FILE=/data/flows.jsonl    # goflow2 sink file

# Alerting — Pushover (https://pushover.net)
# PUSHOVER_USER_KEY=<your user key>
# PUSHOVER_API_TOKEN=<your application token>
PUSHOVER_ENABLED=0             # 1 = real pushes; 0 = log-only (dev)
PUSHOVER_COOLDOWN_S=1800       # per-source alert cooldown
PUSHOVER_MAX_PER_HOUR=20       # global hourly cap

# Models
MODEL_DIR=/models

# ── Disk caps ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ceilings for the three components that grow. Zeek rotates hourly and deletes
# NOTHING on its own, so without a cap the sensor fills its disk, stops
# capturing, and takes ClickHouse down with it -- which surfaces as a database
# fault rather than a capacity one.
#
# Enforced by the weirvern-prune CronJob. Run `weirvern disk` for values sized to
# this host, and `weirvern prune --dry-run` to see what a cap would free.
#
# 0 = no cap. Reported as unset, never as "within budget".
ZEEK_MAX_MB=2048
NETFLOW_MAX_MB=1024
ML_MAX_MB=2048
